# Project structure

This backend is **layered**, not modular. Split a feature across the existing folders. Do not add `app/modules/` or nest models, schemas, routes, and services inside a feature package.

**This layout takes precedence over design specs.** If a spec proposes `app/modules/` or any other feature-package tree, ignore that part and place files here. After implementing, update the spec’s folder section to match — do not copy the spec’s layout into the codebase.

```
app/
  auth/       # JWT, HTTP Bearer, get_current_user — authn/authz only
  config/     # Settings, DB engine/session
  models/     # SQLAlchemy entities (singular): user.py, identity.py, profile.py
  routes/     # FastAPI routers, one file per URL prefix: me.py, profiles.py
  schemas/    # Pydantic request/response models for that API surface
  services/   # Business logic and vendor clients: identity.py, profile.py, cloudinary_client.py
```

## Adding a feature (e.g. matches)

- `app/models/match.py` — tables and enums
- `app/schemas/match.py` — request/response models
- `app/services/match.py` — business rules
- `app/routes/matches.py` — router; include it from `app/main.py`
- Re-export new models from `app/models/__init__.py` so Alembic can see them
- Tests in `tests/test_<feature>.py`, importing from the layered paths

```python
# ❌ BAD — feature package
app/modules/matches/models.py
from app.modules.matches.service import create_match

# ✅ GOOD
app/models/match.py
from app.services.match import create_match
```

Keep routers thin: validate via schemas, call a service, return. Do not put SQLAlchemy queries in `routes/`. Cross-feature calls go service → service (e.g. identity calling `get_or_create_profile`).
